6. SUPPORT MODEL

Before go-live, the implementing engineering team must define and obtain approval for a formal production support model for Singularity Flow (SGOS). The model identifies ownership, operational responsibilities, and escalation paths for all runtime surfaces, governance engines, and enterprise integrations.

Minimum support-model content expected in the readiness package:

Service ownership and on-call: Named primary and secondary owners for the VS Code Extension UI (My Work & Workflow Studio), GitHub Copilot Skill Bridge (/sf-*), Deterministic Node.js CLI & Governance Engine (sflow), Git Branch & Orphan Capability Ledger Store (sflow/config and sflow/state), and critical integrations (Git Remote Host, Skills & Workflow Marketplace, Jira/Enterprise Trackers, and CI/Quality Toolchains).
Incident response: Severity definitions (SEV-1 through SEV-4), response time objectives (RTO/SLA), communication channels, and escalation paths to Git platform administration, identity/SSO, AI/Copilot platform, and enterprise integration teams.
Runbooks: Standard operating procedures for interrupted publication recovery (sflow recover / retained publication replay), orphan sflow/state capability ledger compare-and-swap (CAS) reconciliation, AI/Copilot outage switchover (--no-model manual authorship mode), corrupted local cache/worktree cleanup (sflow doctor), and Git/Jira credential or token/auth failures.
Monitoring and alerting: Operational health indicators (sflow doctor repository health checks, machine-local redacted structured activity logs under .git/ from error to trace, Flow Impact study telemetry, and CI release-gate status), alert thresholds, ownership, and escalation criteria.
Change management: Release cadence for the signed CLI/VS Code extension pair (docs/RELEASE-ARTIFACT-HANDOFF.md), versioned workflow configuration updates on sflow/config (with Workflow Studio simulation preview before activation), rollback procedures for runtime packages and repository workflow definitions, and the pre-release validation checklist (npm run poc:release-gate).
Support handoff: Transition criteria from implementation to steady-state platform support, including Workflow Architect / Tech Lead training, runbook verification, and operations sign-off.
Outage Impact and Fallback Process

Because Singularity Flow executes its deterministic SDLC governance engine locally inside the developer's repository clone and uses Git itself as the authoritative system of record, there is no centralized control-plane database whose outage can halt local engineering work. Instead, outage scenarios involve degradation of upstream/downstream dependencies (Git Remote Host, GitHub Copilot / LLM provider, Skills & Workflow Marketplace, or Jira). During any external outage, teams can continue authoring code, specifications, and tests locally in Git with full deterministic gate validation.

Expected downstream effects during outage:

Git Remote Host Outage (GitHub / GitLab): Local phase authoring, quality checks, and local commits continue normally, but remote Story branch publication, cross-machine Story discovery, and atomic updates to the shared sflow/state capability ledger are temporarily deferred.
GitHub Copilot / LLM Service Outage: Automated AI drafting via /sf-* skills is unavailable; however, phase gate evaluation, prompt composition, and verification remain 100% operational via the deterministic CLI and VS Code UI.
Skills & Workflow Marketplace Outage: Importing new approved workflows, skills, or agent capability packs into Workflow Studio is temporarily unavailable; existing workflows and skills already committed to .singularityflow/ and sflow/config continue to function unaffected.
Jira / Enterprise Tracker Outage: Automated Epic/Story binding lookups and status synchronization are delayed, requiring local Story operations to proceed against cached workspace bindings until connectivity returns.

Fallback process expectation:

Activate incident process and notify impacted stakeholders (delivery teams, Tech Leads / Approvers, and dependent Git/Copilot/Jira platform teams).
Switch to deterministic offline / model-free fallback mode where applicable:
If Copilot / AI is degraded: Continue Story progression using strict --no-model mode (singularity-flow <command> --no-model) and governed manual artifact publication (docs/MODEL-INDEPENDENCE.md).
If Git Remote is degraded: Continue local Story commits and allow Singularity Flow to record durable work-branch publication intents locally without forcing unverified bypasses.
Continue delivery-tool execution of work (local Git branch authoring, isolated qualityCommands test execution, and portable review bundle generation) while recording any emergency manual governance sign-offs with commit-bound rationale.
After service restoration, reconcile deferred state: Run Singularity Flow recovery/synchronization (sflow doctor / retained publication replay) to push queued Story branch commits and reconcile missing sflow/state capability ledger entries from durable work-branch intents.
Execute the post-recovery verification checklist (sflow doctor, sflow status, and spec-to-code delivery receipt verification) to confirm governance state, tree-hash integrity, and approval chains are consistent across main, Story branches, and sflow/state.
Publish an incident summary including outage scope, duration, ledger/branch reconciliation actions taken, and any corrective workflow or tooling measures.
